      heading: "Tundra plants, permafrost and the short summer",
      body:
        "The **tundra** is a cold, treeless region around the edge of the Arctic Ocean — in northern Canada, Alaska, Greenland, northern Scandinavia and Siberia. It covers roughly a tenth of the Earth's land. Winters are long, dark and as cold as −30°C to −40°C; summers are short and cool, with the Sun barely setting (the **midnight sun**).\n\nUnder most of the tundra lies **permafrost** — ground that stays frozen (at or below 0°C) for at least **two years in a row**. Only the top layer, the **active layer**, thaws in summer. Because meltwater cannot drain down through the frozen ground, the summer tundra becomes boggy, with thousands of shallow pools.\n\nPlant problems: freezing temperatures, a **growing season** (the period warm enough for plants to grow) of only about **50–60 days**, strong drying winds, thin, poor soils and frozen ground that roots cannot get into.\n\n**How tundra plants are adapted:**\n\n- **Low-growing, small and close to the ground** — out of the strongest wind, where the air is a little warmer and snow covers them like a blanket in winter.\n- **Shallow roots** — they only need to reach the thin active layer above the permafrost.\n- **Grow in clumps or cushions** to shelter one another and trap warmth.\n- **Dark or reddish leaves** absorb more of the Sun's heat; some have **hairy stems** to trap warm air.\n- **Fast life cycle** — they flower and make seeds quickly in the short summer. The **Arctic poppy** turns its cup-shaped flower to follow the Sun, warming the centre and attracting insects.\n- Many reproduce by **runners or budding** rather than relying on seeds, which may not ripen in time.\n- **Mosses** and **lichens** survive almost anywhere. A **lichen** is a partnership between a **fungus** and an **alga**: the alga makes food by photosynthesis and the fungus holds water and grips bare rock. Lichens can dry out completely and 'switch back on' when wet. 'Reindeer moss' is really a lichen — the main winter food of reindeer (caribou).\n\nAntarctica is even harsher: it has only **two native flowering plants** (Antarctic hair grass and Antarctic pearlwort); everything else is moss, lichen or algae.",
      diagram: `<svg viewBox="0 0 320 210" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Cross-section of the tundra showing low plants, a thin active layer that thaws in summer and waterlogged pools above permanently frozen permafrost"><defs><marker id="tun-ar" markerWidth="8" markerHeight="8" refX="6" refY="3" orient="auto"><path d="M0,0 L6,3 L0,6 Z" fill="#1e3a8a"/></marker></defs><rect width="320" height="60" fill="#e0f2fe"/><circle cx="280" cy="25" r="12" fill="#fde047"/><text x="230" y="50" font-size="10" fill="#713f12">Low summer Sun</text><rect x="0" y="60" width="320" height="45" fill="#a16207"/><rect x="0" y="105" width="320" height="85" fill="#cbd5e1"/><path d="M0 105 h320" stroke="#1e3a8a" stroke-width="2" stroke-dasharray="6 3"/><ellipse cx="200" cy="61" rx="34" ry="5" fill="#60a5fa"/><g fill="#15803d"><ellipse cx="40" cy="58" rx="16" ry="6"/><ellipse cx="90" cy="57" rx="10" ry="7"/><ellipse cx="130" cy="58" rx="14" ry="5"/><ellipse cx="260" cy="58" rx="12" ry="6"/></g><circle cx="90" cy="48" r="4" fill="#facc15"/><g stroke="#422006"><line x1="40" y1="63" x2="40" y2="85"/><line x1="90" y1="63" x2="90" y2="90"/><line x1="130" y1="63" x2="130" y2="82"/></g><text x="10" y="80" font-size="10" fill="#fff7ed">Active layer: thaws in summer</text><text x="10" y="96" font-size="10" fill="#fff7ed">(about 0.3–1 m deep)</text><text x="10" y="128" font-size="10" fill="#0f172a">PERMAFROST: frozen all year (0°C or below</text><text x="10" y="141" font-size="10" fill="#0f172a">for 2+ years) — can be hundreds of metres deep</text><line x1="200" y1="70" x2="200" y2="102" stroke="#1e3a8a" stroke-width="2" marker-end="url(#tun-ar)"/><text x="210" y="88" font-size="10" fill="#fff7ed">Water can't drain</text><text x="210" y="100" font-size="10" fill="#fff7ed">→ boggy pools</text><text x="10" y="178" font-size="10" fill="#0f172a">Shallow roots stay in the active layer; low plants hide from the wind.</text></svg>`,
      diagramCaption:
        "A tundra cross-section: a thin active layer thaws each summer above permanently frozen ground, so the surface becomes waterlogged and roots stay shallow.",
      keyPoints: [
        "Permafrost = ground frozen at or below 0°C for at least two consecutive years.",
        "The active layer thaws in summer; water can't drain, so the tundra becomes boggy.",
        "Tundra plants are low, shallow-rooted, clumped and quick to flower in a 50–60 day growing season.",
      ],
      thinkDeeper:
        "Permafrost contains huge amounts of frozen dead plant material. What happens to that material if the permafrost thaws — and why might that speed up global warming even more?",
      discovery: {
        problem:
          "In the Arctic summer the sun shines for almost 24 hours a day, yet no trees grow on the tundra — only plants a few centimetres high. Suggest two reasons why tall trees cannot survive there.",
        idea:
          "Frozen ground (permafrost) stops deep roots, the growing season is too short to build a woody trunk, and strong, icy winds would dry out and break anything tall. Small, low, shallow-rooted plants are the winners.",
      },
